{#
  This node's VXLAN tunnel endpoint address.

  The VTEP CR is deliberately in Unmanaged mode, and this policy is why.

  In Managed mode OVN-Kubernetes allocates a VTEP IP per node out of the CR's
  CIDRs, which is the right choice on a real fabric where the underlay
  already has a route to the whole block. This lab's fabric is three FRR
  containers with no IGP, so something has to give leaf1 a route to each
  VTEP - and with Managed allocation you do not know which node got which
  address until after the fact.

  Unmanaged inverts that: this policy puts a known address on a known node
  (100.64.0.<the node's usual octet>, same scheme as every other address in
  this lab), OVN-Kubernetes discovers it, and the fabric can carry static
  /32s written at render time. Deterministic, and one less moving part when
  a tunnel does not form.

  A dummy interface rather than an address on the fabric NIC: the VTEP
  address is the node's identity in the overlay, not an interface address,
  and it must stay up and reachable independently of any one link. This is
  the same shape as a router loopback and is what OVN-Kubernetes expects to
  discover.

  Exactly one address per node may match the VTEP CR's CIDRs - if a node has
  two, the VTEP CR goes to a failed status rather than picking one.
#}
apiVersion: nmstate.io/v1
kind: NodeNetworkConfigurationPolicy
metadata:
  name: vtep-{{ node_entry.ip_key }}
spec:
  nodeSelector:
    kubernetes.io/hostname: {{ node_entry.node }}
  desiredState:
    interfaces:
      - name: {{ udn_bgp_vtep_iface }}
        type: dummy
        state: up
        ipv4:
          enabled: true
          dhcp: false
          address:
            - ip: {{ udn_bgp_vtep_prefix }}.{{ ip_list[node_entry.ip_key] }}
              prefix-length: 32
        ipv6:
          enabled: false
